# Game commentary source.

# Formatting:
# [*LINK]: Jump to a new note; also display source if possible. (Default behavior.)
# [~LINK]: Display source for LINK, but keep this note open.
# [text|*LINK], [text|~LINK]: Specify link text.
# [text|https://...] External link.
# [TITLE|relative:../GAME/#RTN:GO] Sibling game link.

ABOUT: Welcome to the commentary track!

rtn:V-VERSION: Print the title, release, and serial number.
	In 1982, Infocom briefly branded its games as “INTERLOGIC”. They had mostly switched to “interactive fiction” by 1984.

GRAMMAR-LEGEND: This is the list of verb forms which the game recognizes. (Simple directions like `NORTH` are a special case; they’re not listed here.) These are compiled from the [SYNTAX|~src:SYNTAX-50] file.
	Objects in a verb line have various flags which give hints about their usage. An object attribute (like `CLIMBBIT`) means that if the player doesn’t name an object, the parser will try to guess one with that attribute.
	The other flag letters have specific meanings: **m**ultiple objects are accepted; the player must **h**ave the object; the player will automatically **t**ake the object; the object should be **i**n the room or **o**n the ground; the object should be **p**ossessed or **c**arried in a container. (Yes, they’re confusing. Infocom’s own docs say “No one really understands them except Stu” (Galley).)

attr:RMUNGBIT: Not applied to real objects. `RMUNGBIT` is a hack used in grammar lines; it means the object is optional (but the preposition before it is required). This allows the parser to accept commands like [LOOK AROUND|~src:SYNTAX-225].

glob:P-SYNTAX: The grammar line found when the most recent command was parsed. (Except for simple direction commands, which are a special case.) See the Grammar tab.


OBJTREE-LEGEND: In this list, `Rooms` are rooms and `Items` are regular items. (Sometimes portable, sometimes not.)
	`Scen` indicates scenery objects. These exist in multiple places; for example, the [~obj:MONITOR] is visible in all three Monitor rooms.
	`Glob` indicates a global object, which is *always* visible, or at least mentionable. Topics that you can `QUERY ABOUT` are always global.

obj:LOCAL-GLOBALS: This is a container for scenery objects like [~obj:MONITOR], which is visible from many locations in the game.


MAP-LEGEND: *Suspended* uses the same NPC transit logic as *Deadline*. Turn on “Show Transit Layer” to display the movement paths (blue) and sightlines (pink).
	If a robot is moving, a grey marker shows its current destination.
	Note that [obj:UD-TUBE] is a hidden room where the [obj:PEOPLE] start. Robots cannot go there.
	
obj:UD-TUBE: A hidden room where the [obj:PEOPLE] start. It’s west of the [~obj:STERILE-AREA], but robots cannot go there.


src:GLOBALS-26: This refers to the word `MGCKJK` in [~obj:LOCAL-GLOBALS], above.
	Why is this synonym necessary? Not clear! Most Infocom games have it (or a variation), but some say “No, this synonym doesn’t need to exist” instead.
	If you type `EXAMINE MGCKJK`, the parser finds [~obj:local-globals]. But that object is nameless, so you get an empty or garbage reply. Go on, try `AUDA, EXAMINE MGCKJK`.

obj:ME: Notionally you, the corpsicle (I mean Central Mentality), are inside the [column|~obj:HOME]. But nothing can directly interact with you; opening the column ends the game immediately. You can only `QUERY ABOUT ME`. So `ME` exists as a global object.

rtn:CRETIN: This is the action routine for [obj:ME]. The name is a remnant of the original Zork games, in which the printed name of the [ADVENTURER|relative:../zork1/#OBJ:ADVENTURER] was “cretin.”

glob:WINNER: `WINNER` is the actor in the current command.
	For a `BOTH` command, `WINNER` is briefly [set|~src:MAIN-170] to the [~obj:TWOBOTS] object. It’s reset to [glob:OLD-WINNER] at the end of the turn, though, so you won’t see this in the State tab.

glob:OLD-WINNER: The previous [glob:WINNER] value. This is needed, for example, to reset the `WINNER` after a `BOTH` command. 

glob:WINNER-HERE: The location of the [glob:WINNER]. 

obj:TWOBOTS: During a `BOTH` command, `WINNER` is briefly [set|~src:MAIN-170] to `TWOBOTS`. It’s reset to [glob:OLD-WINNER] at the end of the turn, though, so you won’t see this in the State tab.
	*Bug:* The [~RTN:TWOBOTS-FCN] routine never checks that the two named robots are *different*. You can therefore complete any two-bot action with just one robot: `BOTH WALDO AND WALDO, ...`.

glob:ROFF: Indicates the robot you are currently commanding. This is a numeric index, not an object value. See [glob:NAME-TBL], [prop:ROBOT].

glob:NAME-TBL: The array of robots in [glob:ROFF] order. (With [obj:PEOPLE] as the seventh entry.) This actually lists object values; the names are in [glob:ROBOT-NAMES].

glob:ROBOT-NAMES: The array of robot names, in [glob:ROFF] order. The robots’ object values are in [glob:NAME-TBL].

glob:DRAG-TBL: This table indicates which (incapacitated) robot is being dragged by a given (active) robot.

glob:FOLLOW-TBL: This table indicates which robot is being `FOLLOW`ed by a given robot. (Not the same as [glob:ROBOT-FOLLOW]!)

glob:ROBOT-FOLLOW: This table indicates which robot a given robot has been told to `GO TO`. (Not the same as [glob:FOLLOW-TBL]! The name is confusing. “ROBOT-SEEKING” would have been clearer.)

glob:LAST-ROOM: As the comment says, a table used by `FOLLOW`.

glob:DEADBOTS: Indicates which robots are suffering from acid damage. The value is [set to 1|~src:GOAL-311] when a robot encounters acid; after 30 turns, the robot dies.

rtn:SET-LAST-ROOM: Set up the [glob:LAST-ROOM] table (all robot locations) at the beginning of play.

glob:HERE: In most Infocom games, `HERE` is the location of the [glob:WINNER]. In *Suspended*, `HERE` is the robot you are currently commanding. (Technically this is based on [glob:ROFF] rather than [glob:WINNER].)
	Why the difference? Because the interpreter always displays `HERE` in top left of the status line, and in *Suspended*, that is a robot name.
	
glob:SCORE: The number of kilodeaths this turn, as calculated in [rtn:I-SCORE].
	In *Deadline* the interpreter was customized to display the `SCORE` and `HERE` variables as a clock time. No such attempt has been made here; you have to read the manual to learn that the “Score” going up is *bad*.
	(A later update to the Z-machine allowed the game to print any text it liked in the status line. A [development version|https://github.com/historicalsource/suspended/blob/master/status.zil] of the *Suspended* source took advantage of this, but it was never released.)

glob:TOTAL-SCORE: The accumulated value of [glob:SCORE] each turn. Which is to say, the total surface death count, in thousands.


prop:ROBOT: Each robot has a numeric value from 1 to 6. This is used to look up values in arrays, such as the [prop:OBJDESCS] and [prop:ROOMDESCS] properties.
	Similarly, the [obj:PEOPLE] have the value 7.

prop:OBJDESCS: This property is a fount of information about how different robots see an object. It’s divided into three groups of six values, plus one more at the end.
	The first group indicates how an object is initially described in the room. 1 means it has no special description and will appear in the general object list. 0 means the robot cannot perceive this object at all.
	The second group gives the object’s common name for each robot. (These may all be the same.)
	The third group is the object’s description when `EXAMINE`d.
	Finally, the optional 19th value is the response if Poet `TOUCH`es the object.

prop:ROOMDESCS: This property describes how each robot perceives each room. If 0, the robot just says “I am in the *room*.”

prop:CLC-TXT: This property contains the responses when Whiz queries the Technical, Advisory, or Historical Peripherals. (The Index Peripheral just indicates which of the other three have responses.)


glob:LIT: Whether the current location has light. (They all do.) Set by calling the [rtn:LIT?] routine.

rtn:LIT?: Determine whether a given location has light. This is true if the room or anything in it has [attr:ONBIT], or if [~glob:ALWAYS-LIT] is set.
	This is a stock bit of Infocom parser code; it is used to set the [glob:LIT] variable. In *Suspended* it always returns true.
	(It would be simpler if [~glob:ALWAYS-LIT] was set, but *Suspended* doesn’t do this.)

glob:LOAD-MAX: The weight limit that the current robot is allowed to carry. This changes every time a robot executes a command.

glob:NUM-EXT: The number of arms on the current robot, and thus the maximum number of items it can carry. This changes every time a robot executes a command.

glob:LOW-DIRECTION: In most games, properties with this value and above are directions.
	In *Suspended*, the `VALUE`, `CAPACITY`, and `SIZE` properties are defined first (see [here|~src:SUSPENDED-35]). So those properties have the highest values. `LOW-DIRECTION` is never checked, however, so this doesn’t cause a problem.

attr:ONBIT: Set for permanently-illuminated rooms. *Suspended* has no darkness, so this is all of them.

attr:RLANDBIT: Set for all rooms.

attr:VILLAIN: Set for living beings ([obj:ME] and [obj:PEOPLE]).

attr:VICBIT: Set for living beings and robots.

attr:CUTBIT: Set for cut cables and robots which are out of commission.

glob:P-WON: Set if the parser successfully parsed a command. (As opposed to a blank line or an error.)
	No relation to whether the *player* has won.

glob:C-INTS: Indicates how much of the timer table ([*glob:C-TABLE]) is used. This counts down from `C-TABLELEN`, which is 180.

glob:C-DEMONS: Indicates how much of the timer table is used for daemons.
	The difference between daemons and timers is that timers run every turn; daemons run every *input*, even if no turn passes. But *Suspended* does not use daemons.

glob:C-TABLE: The timer table. See the Timers tab for a current listing.

TIMERS-LEGEND: A list of the timers running (or paused) in the timer table ([~glob:C-TABLE]). Positive numbers count down (while enabled) and call the named function after that many turns. Negative numbers are called every turn while the timer is enabled.
	This list only grows as you play; timers can be added, started, and stopped, but not removed.

rtn:GO: Set up the world at the start of the game. Start the timers. Print the banner. Set all those useful globals like [~glob:HERE] and [~glob:WINNER]. Do the inital `ARL` command.

rtn:JIGS-UP: Handles the abrupt death of a robot. Sets the [attr:CUTBIT] flag and marks the robot as terminal in [glob:DEADBOTS].
	(Acid damage is handled in the individual robot action routines.)

rtn:ZPROB: This function is never called.

src:MACROS-73: Most Infocom games use a certain amount of randomness to vary the play experience between run-throughs. *Suspended*, in contrast, uses almost no randomness at all. Timers all run on deterministic schedules. If you follow the same script on two different runs, you’ll get the same results.
	The only randomization (aside from quips like [~glob:R-YUKs]) is the reset code selection in [~rtn:GENERATE-CODES]. (Although see [glob:FUMBLE-NUMBER].)
	
glob:FUMBLE-NUMBER: This game inherits the Zork mechanic where carrying too many objects gives a random chance of fumbling. However, this only applies when carrying more than `FUMBLE-NUMBER` objects, which is seven. And no robot has eight arms! So this never happens.

src:PARSER-1087: The parser in *Suspended* is somewhat diegetic. Messages that are normally out-of-world are phrased as if they came from the Filtering Computers. “FC: I don’t know the word ...”
	But when you say `WHIZ, QUERY ABOUT ...`, you’re querying the Computer Library Core, so there’s a whole new set of parser messages. Disambiguation starts with this line, and proceeds to one of the [glob:CROWDED] or [glob:NOT-FOUND] messages.
	The commented-out lines would force the game clock to advance. Apparently they considered the idea of *actually* making you hold on a minute (or a turn).

glob:CROWDED: Messages for successfully resolving an ambiguous object in a `QUERY ABOUT` action. See [this parser code|src:PARSER-1087].

glob:NOT-FOUND: Messages for failing to find an object in a `QUERY ABOUT` action. See [this parser code|src:PARSER-1087].


glob:COR-1: The nine `COR-N` tables define nine corridors of rooms that have sight-lines to each other. Each room in a corridor has that bit set in its [prop:CORRIDOR] property.
	This permits the game to display messages like “Poet is off to the northeast.”

prop:CORRIDOR: This property defines one or more “corridors” (sight-lines) that some robots can see down from a given room. Whiz and Waldo lack distance perception. See [glob:COR-1], [rtn:LOOK-CORRIDOR].

rtn:LOOK-CORRIDOR: Some robots (not Whiz or Waldo!) can see each other at a distance, according to the [prop:CORRIDOR] property.

prop:LINE: The transit line which a given room is on or adjacent to. See [~prop:STATION].

prop:STATION: The transit-line room which is, or is adjacent to, a given room. See [~prop:LINE].

glob:A-LINE: These tables define the six “transit lines” which let robots travel around the map. Every room is either on or adjacent to a transit line.

glob:GOAL-TABLES: The current movement goal of each character. The scheme is explained in the “Travel” tab.

glob:TRANSFER-TABLE: This is effectively a 6x6 table. Each entry is a pair of rooms which connects one “transit line” to another. If two lines don’t cross, the entry gives you the connection to the line that will get you one step closer.
	(The “0 0” pairs are the blank diagonal entries.)

rtn:FOLLOW-GOAL: Move a robot one step towards its current destination (as indicated by [glob:GOAL-TABLES]). [rtn:I-FOLLOW] calls this for each robot in motion.
	If the robot has reached its goal, call [rtn:ARRIVAL]. Otherwise, figure out which room is next (using [glob:TRANSFER-TABLE] and the [transit lines|glob:A-LINE]), and call [rtn:MOVE-RBT].
	
rtn:MOVE-RBT: Move a robot, and also any robot which is assigned to `FOLLOW` it. Calls [rtn:MOVE-RBT?] to do the dirty work.

rtn:MOVE-RBT?: A low-level routine which tries to move an individual robot. It checks for impassable exits, which means it can fail. Thus the question mark.
	This calls the even lower-level [rtn:DO-THE-MOVE] routine to handle the actual object juggling.
	This is called both by the transit-line system ([rtn:MOVE-RBT]) and by individual direction commands ([~rtn:GOTO]).

rtn:DO-THE-MOVE: The lowest-level routine for moving an individual robot. This places the robot in the destination room. If the robot is dragging another (incapacitated) robot, this also moves the dragee. (See [glob:DRAG-TBL].)
	This is called by [rtn:MOVE-RBT?]

rtn:ARRIVAL: Called when a moving robot reaches its destination. Clears its [glob:GOAL-TABLES] entry, then calls [~rtn:G-ROBOT] to announce the arrival.


obj:PEOPLE: The group of humans who enter the Complex. They hang around “off-stage” in [obj:UD-TUBE] until it’s time for them to arrive.
	If you `COUNT HUMANS`, you can discover that there are two of them. Otherwise, the game never says.

glob:PR-NUMBER: The state variable that determines what the [obj:PEOPLE] are doing.

glob:SLEEPERS: The counter for the [obj:PEOPLE]’s nap. See [rtn:I-STEAL].

rtn:I-PEOPLE1: This timer triggers the initial arrival of the [obj:PEOPLE]. They will go to the [~obj:SLEEP-CHAMBER] first (see [rtn:I-STEAL]).

rtn:I-STEAL: This timer increments the [glob:SLEEPERS] counter as the [obj:PEOPLE] take a nap in the [~obj:SLEEP-CHAMBER].
	If a robot grabs the [~obj:TOOLBAG], they wake up and chase it ([rtn:I-CHASEAUDA]). Otherwise, after 20 turns, they awaken and head to the [~obj:INNER-CORE] ([rtn:I-PEOPLE2]).

rtn:I-CHASEAUDA: Handles the [obj:PEOPLE] chasing a robot who has grabbed the [~obj:TOOLBAG]. The function name is misleading; it can be any robot ([~glob:RTHIEF]).
	If you lead the humans to observe the acid leak, they’ll fix it and then head to the [~obj:REC-AREA]. Otherwise, they’ll eventually catch up with you, get the toolbag back, and return to their original plan ([rtn:I-PEOPLE2]).

rtn:I-PEOPLE2: Send the [obj:PEOPLE] off to the [~obj:INNER-CORE]. When they arrive, they will spend a few turns looking up replacement procedures ([rtn:I-PEOPLE4]).

rtn:I-PEOPLE4: Handles the [obj:PEOPLE] looking up info in the [~obj:INNER-CORE]. When they’re done, they’ll head to the [~obj:HALL-END] to use the transit car ([rtn:I-CAR-WAIT]).

rtn:I-CAR-WAIT: If the car is available, the [obj:PEOPLE] will take it to the bio area and begin reviving a replacement clone ([rtn:I-KILL]).
	If a robot is camping in the car or has moved it to the far end, the humans will wait up to 75 turns. After that they give up and head towards the [~obj:CPU-ROOM] to shut you down, clone or no clone. (See [rtn:PARRIVE7].)
	
rtn:I-KILL: Handles the [obj:PEOPLE] working to revive a clone. This takes 15 turns. After that, they’re on their way back to the [~obj:CPU-ROOM] to shut you down. (See [rtn:PARRIVE5].)

rtn:PARRIVE5: This travel callback executes when the [obj:PEOPLE] reach the [~obj:CPU-ROOM] with your clone in tow. More precisely, it executes *you*.

rtn:PARRIVE7: This travel callback executes when the [obj:PEOPLE] reach the [~obj:CPU-ROOM] without a clone. You still die, of course.

rtn:I-STARCROSS: Handles the [obj:PEOPLE] waiting in the [~obj:REC-AREA]. They’ve just fixed the acid leak and are giving you a chance to repair everything else.
	After 200 turns, they give up and head to the [~obj:INNER-CORE]. See [rtn:I-PEOPLE4].
	They spend their down-time playing *Starcross*. 200 turns is definitely not enough time to win *Starcross*.

glob:PLAY-STARCROSS: A counter for how long the [obj:PEOPLE] have played *Starcross* in the [~obj:REC-AREA]. See [rtn:I-STARCROSS].


rtn:I-SCORE: This timer works out how many surface humans die every turn. It sets [glob:SCORE] and increases [glob:TOTAL-SCORE] by that value.
	This includes a call to [rtn:DO-RTD], which handles the transit part of the death count.
	If you manage to kill 30 million people, the game gives up on you. (See [~rtn:ALL-DEAD].)

rtn:I-FOLLOW: This timer handles ongoing robot movement. See [rtn:FOLLOW-GOAL].

rtn:I-IRIS-HINTS: Iris periodically reminds you that she needs her visuals fixed.

rtn:I-AUDA-HINTS: Auda reacts the first time the [obj:PEOPLE] enter her location.

rtn:I-TREMOR1: The first tremor causes the acid leak.

rtn:I-TREMOR2: The second tremor perturbs the Hydroponics settings.
	Transit deaths begin at the same time, but that’s not handled by this routine.

rtn:I-ROBOTKILLER: This timer handles progressive acid damage by incrementing the [glob:DEADBOTS] counters.

rtn:I-WIRE-MESSAGE: This timer nudges you about the reset codes once you’ve successfully repaired both cable conduits.

rtn:I-IMP: `IMPOSSIBLE` mode is of course a joke. The star goes nova in two turns and there’s nothing you can do about it.
	I don’t know if the wonderful game *Outer Wilds* (2019) was inspired by this. I hope so.
	I know that *I* was inspired to add an `IMPOSSIBLE` mode to my game *The Dreamhold* (2004). Although – *spoiler* – in my game, it’s not truly impossible!


glob:RTD-KILLS: The turn on which the food and transit systems go haywire. This is synced with [rtn:I-TREMOR2], which displays the message about damage to those systems.
	`RTD-KILLS` is normally 75, but is reduced to 2 in `ADVANCED` mode. (A few places in the code compare [~glob:MOVES] to the literal 75 instead of `RTD-KILLS`. This probably means bugs in `ADVANCED` mode.)

rtn:DO-RTD: Add transit deaths to [glob:SCORE]. This is part of the [rtn:I-SCORE] timer. It’s also invoked if you flip the switches in [Transit Control|~obj:RTD]; this ensures that the transit death rate drops (or rises!) immediately.
	The logic is straightforward. Taxi and floater usage go up every turn as long as the switches are on. If the turn is past [glob:RTD-KILLS], there are crashes (although these aren’t really connected to the usage values). Turning off the floater switch immediately kills everyone in the floaters, but prevents further deaths.
	As for the ramps, they accelerate wildly and kill people. Turning off the switch brings the speed back down, although not instantly.

rtn:I-FOOD: Adjust the food situation every turn. This is a simple simulation: the [~glob:WATER-LEVEL], [~glob:MINERALS], and [~glob:WATTS] (lighting) variables are compared with their optimal values. This turn’s food production ([glob:FOOD-TONS]) is 100 minus the total (absolute) error of those three variables.

glob:FOOD-TONS: How much food is produced by hydroponics this turn. The optimal value is 100. If it falls below 50, some people die of starvation this turn. If it reaches zero, [everybody dies|~src:ROBOTS-612], o the embarrassment.

rtn:I-DECAY: Slowly increase the [glob:DECAY] value over time.

glob:DECAY: The amount that the second weather control dial ([~obj:WDIAL2]) is off-kilter. See [rtn:I-WEATHER].

rtn:I-WEATHER: Adjust the three tower pressure values ([~glob:PRESSURE1], [~glob:PRESSURE2], [~glob:PRESSURE3]) based on the dial settings. (Note that dial 2 has an error of [glob:DECAY], which increases slowly over time.) Then adjust the [glob:WINDS] and [glob:DEGREES] (temperature) based on the pressure values.
	The logic is that [glob:WINDS] is the total (absolute) difference between the tower pressures, and [glob:DEGREES] is based on their average. 
	
glob:WINDS: The wind speed on the surface. If this goes over 20, people die. Over 50 is worse.

glob:DEGREES: The surface temperature. Under 40, people die.
	(Apparently Contra uses Fahrenheit, and people are immune to heatstroke.)


src:SUSPENDED-1: The game is titled *Suspension*, not *Suspended*, in most of the source code.
	I got some cheap amusement out of telling my school friends, “I got *Suspended!*” (Which everybody heard as a serious school penalty rather than a game title.)

obj:HELL: This exists only so that you can tell a robot `GO TO HELL`.

src:GOAL-168: There is no `SKY4`. The compiler silently stores a zero in that entry.
	(This seems to be a recurring problem. *Deadline* had a nonexistent `[SHALL-3|relative:../starcross/#SRC:GOAL-37]` in one of its transit tables.)

obj:FC1: This room is missing the exit southwest to [~obj:MIDMIST]. It’s easy to overlook, because the [~glob:C-LINE] connects those rooms, and `GO TO...` commands follow the transit lines rather than the room exits.
	(c:Torbjörn Andersson)

obj:HALL-STOP4: Valid exits exist between this room and the [~obj:REC-AREA] to the north. But they’re not on the original printed map. (I’ve added the connection to the map in the Map tab.)

src:ROBOTS-878: The [obj:PEOPLE] object has two [prop:CLC-TXT] definitions. This is technically illegal but the compiler apparently didn’t report an error.
	Because of the way Z-machine properties work, only the second entry has any effect. The strings in the first entry appear in the game file, but can never be printed. I have greyed them out to indicate this.

obj:SUPPLIES-NORTH: The [prop:ROOMDESCS] entry in this definition is a stub. The room description is dynamic (because the [~obj:MACHINE] might be open or closed), and [prop:ROOMDESCS] doesn’t support that. Instead the description is printed by [~rtn:SUPPLIES-N-FCN], pieced together from [~glob:LDSUPPLIES], [~glob:WIRE-FIRST], and [~glob:WIRE-SECOND].

src:OBJECTS-1205: “Emmak”? I feel like I missed a joke.

obj:DEADBOT: Calling the dead super-bot “Fred” is a minor in-joke. Infocom’s office mainframe was named Fred.

src:PEOPLE-284: The game clearly wants to avoid assumptions about the player’s gender, but these dialogue snippets slip in several places. A [development version|https://github.com/historicalsource/suspended/blob/master/people.zil#L289] of the source code corrects these (“*the* plug”, “*the* clone”). However, that version was never released.

rtn:ADJ-CHECK: This adjective list is copied over from [Starcross|relative:../starcross/#RTN:ADJ-CHECK]!

rtn:V-LOOK-SAFELY: This is the `LOOK AT __ THROUGH __` command, which is copied straight over from [Starcross|relative:../starcross/#RTN:V-LOOK-SAFELY]. *Suspended* has no transparent objects, so the action is useless.
	(The “X isn’t transparent” message is misleading, really. Only Iris would notice! And Waldo’s sonar can penetrate some surfaces, and so on.)
	The action is called `LOOK-SAFELY` because *Starcross* has a laser that can blind you.

src:PARSER-288: This adverb check is copied from [Deadline|relative:../deadline/#SRC:PARSER-265] and [Starcross|relative:../starcross/#SRC:PARSER-269]. It’s only active in *Deadline*, though.

src:PARSER-202: This check for titles is copied from [Deadline|relative:../deadline/#SRC:PARSER-183]. It’s commented out here, since robots always use the informal.

rtn:ENOUGH-TRADING: In this routine definition, the `<` and `>` delimiters are unbalanced. There’s one close-bracket too many.
	Any modern language would scream bloody murder about this kind of mistake, but apparently Infocom’s ZIL compiler silently ignored it. So did the MDL compiler that it was based on. See [discussion|https://intfiction.org/t/question-about-infocom-zil-syntax/80690/9].
